<BlogArticle
	title="PAN Card Photo Size in CM and MM for an Offline Form"
	description="Understand the commonly quoted 3.5 × 2.5 cm PAN paper-form photo size, convert it to millimetres, and check the current Form 93/94 print instructions."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/pan-photo-size-cm-mm/"
	heading="PAN card photo size in cm and mm for the offline form"
	intro="Paper forms use a physical photograph, so the important measurement is its printed width and height—not the image’s KB value. The familiar 3.5 × 2.5 cm size comes from older PAN form guidance; check the current blank form before printing."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">The commonly quoted paper-photo measurement</h2>
		<p>
			Older PAN application guidance commonly describes a passport-size
			photograph as <strong>3.5 × 2.5 cm</strong>, which is
			<strong>35 × 25 mm</strong> (height × width). That gives a photo
			about 1.38 inches tall and 0.98 inches wide. Check the current
			form’s printed photo box and instructions, and match the
			orientation: these measurements are height first, width second.
		</p>
		<p>
			Form 49A is no longer accepted for new PAN applications from
			1 April 2026. Current Indian new-PAN applications use Form 93 or
			Form 94 according to the provider’s official instructions. Do not
			assume that the older 35 × 25 mm guidance automatically applies
			to a current blank form if its photo area or instructions differ.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Print size is not pixel size or KB</h2>
		<p>
			Centimetres and millimetres describe physical dimensions. Pixels
			describe digital resolution, and KB describes how much storage
			the saved file uses. The same 35 × 25 mm photo can have different
			pixel dimensions depending on print resolution. For example, at
			200 DPI, the equivalent is approximately 276 × 197 pixels
			(height × width). That calculation is a conversion, not proof that
			a current form requires 200 DPI or those pixels.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">How to prepare a paper photo</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Download the latest blank form from the provider handling your application.</li>
			<li>Read the photo instructions and measure the designated box; use the form’s specified size if it differs from 35 × 25 mm.</li>
			<li>Print a recent, clear color photo at the required physical dimensions, with the subject framed as instructed.</li>
			<li>Place or affix the photograph only as the current form directs. Avoid covering text, barcodes, or other fields.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			An image tool can prepare the crop and dimensions before printing,
			but the printer’s scaling setting can change the final physical
			size. Print at actual size (100%) and measure a test print with a
			ruler.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		For the latest paper-form information, see the official
		<a class="text-link" href="https://www.pan.utiitsl.com/PAN/newA.html">UTIITSL Form 93/94 page</a>
		or <a class="text-link" href="https://tinpan.proteantech.in/services/pan/pan-index.html">Protean’s PAN application guidance</a>.
		A <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card photo resizer</a>
		can help prepare an image, but the live form controls what to print.
	</p>
</BlogArticle>
